<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ===================================================================================
         V1 BASELINE (SCRUM-107, owner directive 2026-08-08). THIS FILE MINTS man_sbsr_resp
         FOR THE FIRST TIME. There is no earlier shape of it anywhere.

         Every DCRE database is dropped and recreated for the direct cut-over, so no dcre_man
         carries a MAR-era man_sbsr_resp, no checksum for this changelog was ever recorded, and
         nothing needs converging to a current shape. The retrofit apparatus is ABSENT BY
         DESIGN and must not be re-added: no <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum>, no defensive
         IF NOT EXISTS on the CREATE, no additive retrofit changeset moving a migrated table to
         this shape. The final shape is the shape below.

         THE TWO MARK_RAN GUARDS BELOW ARE CONVERGENCE GUARDS, NOT RETROFITS. man_sbsr_resp has
         TWO creators on a brand-new dcre_man: this changelog, and MRG's pre-create
         `004-bootstrap-man-sbsr-resp-mrg` in mrg db/changelog/2026/07/004-man-views.xml, which
         MRG ships because its mnd_sbsr_pick view reads the table and MRG cannot depend on MSX
         having migrated first. All ten M-services migrate the one dcre_man with ten independent
         history + lock tables and NOTHING serializes them, so on an empty database either
         writer can land first. That is exactly the condition a MARK_RAN guard is for.

         MSX is the SBSR leg reader and writes NOTHING else. The isr and pbsr tables moved out
         with their own services (MIX, MPX): one reader per leg, the exact mirror of the
         collections cix/csx/cpx fleet. The merged three-table changelog was MAR's shape and is
         the deviation SCRUM-91 removed, so the reply.type launch arg is gone too: the leg is a
         compile-time property of the service.

         One row per reply file (a pain.012 message carries exactly ONE mandate, unlike the
         collections ISR fan-out). Replay-safe and resume-safe via UNIQUE
         (response_file, mndt_req_id): the FULL business identity of a landed reply row (the
         file it came in plus the mandate it answers), so the guarded
         INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (response_file, mndt_req_id) DO NOTHING is a zero-duplicate
         no-op on re-run (never UPSERT on the PK; CRDB resolves UPSERT on PK only,
         persistence.md).
         orgnl_msg_id = the outbound MsgId MSX correlates to man_outbound.out_msg_id
         (fail-closed: an unknown outbound identity is WARNed and excluded, never guessed,
         SCRUM-60 canon). mndt_id = the MndtId (mandate_ref), mndt_req_id = the MRR-minted
         MndtReqId. e2e is nullable-but-forward-compatible: the current synthetic pain.012
         contract (A-60) carries no EndToEndId element, so it is parsed opportunistically and
         stays NULL until the reply shape carries it. status/reason are the literal MndtSts/Rsn
         code values (R-21: code values, never enum ordinals).
         =================================================================================== -->

    <changeSet id="msx-001-man-sbsr-resp" author="dcre">
        <!-- CONVERGENCE GUARD: MRG's 004-bootstrap-man-sbsr-resp-mrg creates this same table
             and nothing orders the two services. See the file header. -->
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="man_sbsr_resp"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <createTable tableName="man_sbsr_resp">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="response_file" type="VARCHAR(128)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="orgnl_msg_id" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="mndt_id" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="mndt_req_id" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="e2e" type="VARCHAR(35)"/>
            <column name="status" type="VARCHAR(8)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="reason" type="VARCHAR(8)"/>
            <column name="version" type="BIGINT" defaultValueNumeric="0"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="updated_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- The unique constraint is its OWN changeset guarded on its OWN schema state
         (persistence.md: guard each step on the schema state it transforms, not on file
         order), and on a v1 database that is load-bearing in two ways.

         First, MRG creates the table AND this constraint in ONE changeset gated on
         tableExists alone. So when MSX wins the race and creates the table, MRG's whole
         changeset MARK_RANs, the constraint never lands from MRG, and THIS changeset is its
         only remaining writer. Folded into the createTable changeset above, the single
         tableExists gate would MARK_RAN both statements together on any database that already
         holds the table, and every runtime ON CONFLICT (response_file, mndt_req_id) would then
         die with "there is no unique or exclusion constraint matching the ON CONFLICT
         specification": the zero-duplicate idempotency guarantee silently gone.

         Second, the two changesets commit separately, so a pod killed between them leaves the
         table standing without the constraint. The restart must repair exactly that state,
         which only a guard probing the constraint itself can see.

         A UNIQUE constraint materialises as an index of the same name, so indexExists is the
         schema-state probe for it. Both states are proven by the fixtures in MsxLegacyStateIT
         (SCRUM-91 review R5). -->
    <changeSet id="msx-001-man-sbsr-resp-uq" author="dcre">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><indexExists tableName="man_sbsr_resp" indexName="uq_man_sbsr_resp_file_mndt_req"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="man_sbsr_resp" columnNames="response_file, mndt_req_id"
                             constraintName="uq_man_sbsr_resp_file_mndt_req"/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
